# nyairo line-ending policy (repository root).
#
# Why this file exists: the v0.2 native recall components are sealed by the sha256 of their
# bytes (`components/native/memory_runtime_v1/production_resolver.py: SEALED_SHA256`), and the
# patch baseline records the digest of `patches/baseline.json`.  A checkout that rewrites text
# files to CRLF therefore fails three ways at once:
#
#   * `RuntimeError: memory resolver unavailable: sealed_component_mismatch:<name>`
#   * `baseline checksum mismatch` from patches/check-baseline.sh
#   * `scripts/bootstrap.sh` cannot even run under bash
#     (`set: pipefail: invalid option name`), so the installer tests go red
#
# One policy for the whole tree fixes all three: text is stored with LF in the repository and
# checked out with LF on every platform, Windows included.  The same one-line rule already
# exists in `website/.gitattributes`; this file applies it to the rest of the tree.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git keep deciding what is text, so the tracked PNG / JPG / ICO / WOFF2 / PDF
# assets are left byte-for-byte alone.  The explicit lines below only make that promise visible
# and cover the file kinds the release toolchain depends on.  `vendor/hermes/.gitattributes`
# stays in charge inside the vendored tree (its `*.ps1 text eol=crlf` is deliberate).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell, Python and configuration text: LF everywhere.
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.bash     text eol=lf
*.py       text eol=lf
*.pyi      text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.jsonl    text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.toml     text eol=lf
*.cfg      text eol=lf
*.ini      text eol=lf
*.service  text eol=lf
*.patch    text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.txt      text eol=lf
*.ts       text eol=lf
*.tsx      text eol=lf
*.mjs      text eol=lf
*.js       text eol=lf
*.css      text eol=lf
*.html     text eol=lf

# Text files that are stored with CRLF *inside the repository* today.  `-text` means "never
# convert, in either direction", so the bytes on disk stay identical to the bytes in the
# repository on every platform and no `git add` can silently rewrite them.
#
# The first one is load-bearing: `production_resolver.SEALED_SHA256` seals it as
# 6667ff66fe8105f28f436609f0cd8d744b185c0580e7f1642aafef0b873af78a, which is the sha256 of the
# CRLF bytes.  A renormalising `git add` would flip the blob to LF and the v0.2 native recall
# would stop assembling with `sealed_component_mismatch:readonly_recall_adapter.py`.
#
# The other five carry no recorded digest; they are listed so that the stored bytes stay
# deterministic instead of leaving a latent "renormalise" diff for the next batch.
#
# The root `.gitignore` is the one entry that had already drifted: its committed blob is
# mixed (lines 1-9 LF, lines 10-21 CRLF), so `text=auto` leaves it alone on checkout but a
# `git add` would silently flip the whole file to LF.  It is anchored with a leading `/` so
# the rule cannot leak onto the many `.gitignore` files under `vendor/`.
/.gitignore                                                       -text
components/native/native_recall/readonly_recall_adapter.py        -text
MANIFEST.json                                                     -text
components/.gitignore                                             -text
components/alpha/tests/acceptance/e_safety.py                     -text
vendor/hermes/contributors/emails/uperLu@users.noreply.github.com -text

# Assets that must never be normalised.
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.ico      binary
*.woff2    binary
*.pdf      binary
